Subject: fonts: consult the allowlist for the family the platform will match

Patch 0090 tests a page's family name against the profile's enumeration
allowlist exactly as written. On Windows that is the wrong name to test, because
Chromium resolves some family names by stripping a recognised suffix and
matching the base family instead. A host with Arial answers "present" for
"Arial Narrow" whether or not a narrow face is installed, and the same holds for
twenty other suffixes: twelve weight names in TypefacesHasWeightSuffix and nine
stretch names in TypefacesHasStretchSuffix, both in
win/font_cache_skia_win.cc and both reached from CreateFontPlatformData. So an
allowlist naming Arial and not Arial Narrow made 0090 hide a family every real
Windows machine with Arial reports, which is a machine that cannot exist.

The same behaviour exists on Linux and is ours: WindowsStretchSuffix in
skia/font_cache_skia.cc emulates the stretch table for a Windows persona,
guarded on the profile's claimed platform. There is no weight emulation there,
so this mirrors only what is actually emulated. macOS does none of it, and that
is not an omission: CoreText enumerates "Avenir Next" and "Avenir Next
Condensed" as separate families, so hiding one while showing the other is a
claim a Mac can make, and stripping there would decline to hide a family the
profile legitimately excluded.

Fixing this in the catalogue was considered and rejected. Mirroring twenty-one
suffixes per family in data multiplies the non-macOS allowlists by twenty-two
and reopens the hole every time someone adds a family and forgets to add the
variants. It is a code-shaped problem, and the code-shaped fix is a fixed table
consulted once.

So the predicate moves to FontCache::ProfileHidesFamily and gains the strip:
test the name as written, and if that fails, strip one recognised suffix and
test the base. Weight is tried first and precludes stretch, one level only,
because that is the order and the depth of the else-if chain being mirrored.
The suffix strings are duplicated here rather than exported from the platform
font caches, which keeps the matchers themselves untouched; a suffix added
upstream and missed here can only make this filter hide a family it should have
shown, never show one it should have hidden.

Putting it on FontCache also consolidates 0090. The three renderer routes each
carried their own copy of the profile lookup; now they call one function and the
profile is reached from one file in Blink. That is not tidiness. A filter
applied to document.fonts.check() but not to canvas measurement has the two
contradict each other, and the only way to be sure they agree is for there to be
one implementation to disagree with.

This stays subtractive, and the suffix strip does not weaken that. Stripping
decides which allowlist entry to consult; the platform matcher still runs
afterwards and still has to find a face. A permissive decision here can only
decline to hide a family, never make an absent one resolve. That is also why it
cannot introduce a disagreement between routes: both pass the same filter and
then reach the same matcher.

It satisfies the base-versus-suffix coherence rule by construction rather than
by catalogue discipline, and in one direction only. A suffixed name can pass
only by having an allowed base, so an allowed base admits every variant the
platform resolves by stripping, and a variant can never be allowed while its
base is hidden.

queryLocalFonts() is unchanged and needs no strip. Its browser-side filter works
on the platform's enumeration table, whose entries are families that really are
installed, so there is no suffix-derived request to resolve. A Windows host with
Arial and no narrow face already reports "Arial Narrow" as matchable while not
listing it among installed families; that is upstream behaviour and not
something introduced here.

Compile-unverified: this cannot be built in this session.

